I'm still back to the O(n^2) solution with `duplicates.includes(item)`. Let me use a separate Set to track which items have been added to duplicates:

```js
function findDuplicates(arr) {
    const seen = new Set();
    const duplicates = [];
    const addedToDuplicates = new Set();

    for (const item of arr) {
        if (seen.has(item)) {
            if (!addedToDuplicates.has(item)) {
                duplicates.push(item);
                addedToDuplicates.add(item);
            }
        } else {
            seen.add(item);
        }
    }

    return duplicates;
}
```

This solution has O(n) time complexity and O(n) space complexity. Let me explain the complexity:

**Time Complexity:**
- We iterate through the array once: O(n)
- For each element, we perform constant time operations (Set.has, Set.add, Array.push): O(1)
- Overall: O(n) * O(1) = O(n)

**Space Complexity:**
- We use two Sets (seen and addedToDuplicates) and one array (duplicates)
- In the worst case, all elements are unique, so the Sets store n elements
- The duplicates array stores at most n elements
- Overall: O(n) + O(n) + O(n) = O(n)

Now, let me explain how JavaScript Set iteration order works and how this justifies our implementation's ordering claim:

JavaScript Set objects iterate in insertion order. This means that when we add elements to a Set, they will be iterated in the order they were added. In our implementation:

1. We iterate through the input array in order
2. When we encounter an element for the first time, we add it to the `seen` Set
3. When we encounter a duplicate element (one already in `seen`), we add it to the `duplicates` array if it hasn't been added before

Since we process the array in order and only add duplicates when we first discover them (the second occurrence), the `duplicates` array will contain the duplicates in the order they were first discovered.

Let me verify this with some test cases:

```js
